-- The pool (store/) holds objects named by the hash of their content and never changed: a text, a
-- module (its pieces), a root, the names of a root, and beside a checked definition's key its
-- forms (type and term as elaborated, readable back), implicit mask, declaration and text.
-- A name or a path is one machine's label for an object, set by that machine's writes only
-- (.cubical-store/index, bound/). A root is one hash for everything the labels hold; its names
-- are a tree of 256 parts by the hash of a name, each part an object, saying each name's key,
-- text and module. `hyperactive root` says it; `take ROOT` makes the labels hold it;
-- `apply PATH BASE OTHER` builds from another's work as this machine's act. Reading a module
-- moves no other module's label.
-- A definition is taken by its key (src/syntax.c load_key): the keys its forms mention first, then
-- its forms given to the handler of a checked definition, which finds the certificate and does not
-- run the checker. A name already holding the very object being declared is declared once. Of
-- the 30,685 names held (2026-11-28), 26,090 can be taken this way; the rest are the untyped base
-- and primitives, which every reading loads.
-- A declaration is written on its own, in no module (`hyperactive write NAME`; lib/hyperactive
-- hy-free, src/lang.c --keep): checked with what it mentions taken by key, labelled when nothing
-- fails. A module sees such a name. So a new definition needs no module, no order, no "after".
-- The modules that exist are not dissolved.
-- A key does not depend on what was loaded before: the number a code carries for a definition or
-- a data type is the first 60 bits of its key (src/store.c pub_of), where it was the place in
-- the machine's tables. Found by sending a root to a place, taking it in a checkout with no pool,
-- and comparing the root said there with the root sent: they differed until this, the names
-- applied last, and labels unmoved by reading were in; then they were one hash. Not repeated
-- since the last of those changes. The members of a recursive group name one another by place
-- while the group is checked.
-- A place (HYPERACTIVE_PLACE) is a URL that stores bytes under a name and computes nothing.
-- `hyperactive send` stores the pool's new objects as one pack under its hash and, under the
-- root's hash, the packs that hold what it names; `take ROOT` fetches the packs a machine lacks,
-- each kept only when its hash is its name. A machine with no pool fetches the first packs itself
-- (src/lang.c place_first). Shown with a directory as the place.
-- A catch-all clause's right side is copied for each case it covers (src/syntax.c cc_match): one
-- shared node had its implicit arguments solved in the first case and used in the others, so a
-- proof by cases ending `_ _ = refl` was refused.
-- Open: `hyperactive write` keeps a change only when nothing fails in what it touches, so a
-- module broken by its dependencies cannot be repaired a definition at a time; the chain of ∙
-- between large terms refused where the same chain through a lemma is accepted, not reproduced.
